---
import Layout from '../layouts/Layout.astro';
import Nav from '../components/Nav.astro';
import Footer from '../components/Footer.astro';
import '../styles/blogs.css';
---

<Layout title="Engineering Work — Safu" description="Selected backend, AI, machine learning, and quantitative engineering projects by Safu Harry.">
	<main class="blog-page">
		<Nav />

		<header class="blog-hero">
			<div class="blog-hero__identity">
				<img src="/IMG-20210808-WA0072.jpg" alt="Safu Harry" loading="eager" />
				<div>
					<p class="eyebrow">Engineering Journal · Projects · Architecture</p>
					<h1>Work built from <span>first principles.</span></h1>
				</div>
			</div>
			<p>
				These are engineering projects used to explore architecture, reliability, security, AI integration, data
				systems, and reusable software foundations. Each project is presented through the engineering problem it solves,
				the architecture behind it, and the technologies used to implement it.
			</p>
		</header>

		<section class="project-grid" aria-label="Selected projects">
			<article class="project-card">
				<div class="project-card__meta"><span>01 · Backend Architecture</span><span>v0.1.0 Beta</span></div>
				<h2>FastApiBackbone</h2>
				<p class="project-card__lede">
					A packaged, production-oriented foundation for Python backend projects. It extends FastAPI with the
					infrastructure concerns required for durable services: environment-driven configuration, async SQLAlchemy,
					PostgreSQL/SQLite support, Alembic migrations, structured logging, application lifespan management, health
					probes, and a reusable application factory.
				</p>
				<div class="project-card__architecture">FastAPI → API composition → application services
					↓
				configuration + lifecycle + structured logging
					↓
				SQLAlchemy 2.x ↔ PostgreSQL / SQLite
					↓
				Alembic migrations</div>
				<div class="project-card__tags">
					<span>Python</span><span>FastAPI</span><span>SQLAlchemy 2.x</span><span>PostgreSQL</span><span>SQLite</span><span>Alembic</span><span>Pydantic</span><span>structlog</span><span>Async I/O</span>
				</div>
				<a class="project-card__link" href="https://github.com/safuh/FastApiBackbone" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">Inspect the package →</a>
			</article>

			<article class="project-card">
				<div class="project-card__meta"><span>02 · AI Systems</span><span>Active Development</span></div>
				<h2>PAssist</h2>
				<p class="project-card__lede">
					A provider-agnostic AI operating platform built on the same backend principles. PAssist combines a modular
					FastAPI service with identity, JWT authentication, persistent conversations, AI provider abstraction, local
					Ollama integration, retrieval, tools, memory, and extensible domain boundaries.
				</p>
				<div class="project-card__architecture">Identity → JWT authentication → sessions
					↓
				FastAPI application → domain modules
					↓
				AI provider contract → Ollama / compatible APIs
					↓
				SQLAlchemy 2.x → Alembic → PostgreSQL</div>
				<div class="project-card__tags">
					<span>Python</span><span>FastAPI</span><span>JWT</span><span>Argon2</span><span>SQLAlchemy</span><span>PostgreSQL</span><span>Ollama</span><span>LLMs</span><span>RAG</span><span>Tool Calling</span>
				</div>
				<a class="project-card__link" href="https://github.com/safuh/Passist" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">Explore PAssist →</a>
			</article>
		</section>

		<section class="engineering-note" aria-labelledby="note-title">
			<p class="eyebrow">The connection</p>
			<h2 id="note-title">Infrastructure first. Domain second.</h2>
			<p>
				FastApiBackbone is the reusable backend substrate: asynchronous application structure, database lifecycle,
				migrations, configuration, logging, health checks, and clean extension points. PAssist demonstrates how that
				foundation can support a substantially more complex domain with identity, security, AI provider abstraction,
				conversations, tools, knowledge retrieval, memory, and future multi-user capabilities.
			</p>
			<p>
				The distinction matters: FastApiBackbone is intentionally domain-neutral, while PAssist owns product-specific
				behavior. The result is a reusable engineering foundation that can be carried into other Python backend projects
				without importing the assumptions of an AI application.
			</p>
		</section>

		<footer class="footer">
			<Footer />
		</footer>
	</main>
</Layout>
